HIER(7)                  Horus Filesystem Hierarchy                  HIER(7)

NAME
    hier - the layout of the Horus filesystem

DESCRIPTION
    All files live in one encrypted object store, served by the ring-3
    fs_server. On a live boot the store is in memory and is gone at power
    off; on an installed system it is the volume, unlocked by the first
    login.

    THE SYSTEM TREES are rebuilt at every boot from the boot modules the
    kernel verified against the measured manifest. Anything else found in
    them is removed, and every change is refused to every login, the
    administrator's included. A system is updated by new install media,
    never in place.

    /bin            programs, run by name (sh(1))
    /sbin           programs for init or the administrator
    /lib            reserved for shared libraries (the shared C library
                    is held by the kernel and handed out by capability, so
                    it is not a file here)
    /usr            read-only data
    /usr/share/man  this manual, one plain-text file per page (man(1))
    /usr/share/doc  licence texts and the source offer for the GNU and
                    TCC programs shipped here

    THE REST belongs to the administrator and the accounts, and persists
    on an installed system:

    /etc            configuration: fs.policy(5)
    /home           one directory per account, owned by it, mode 0700,
                    made at the first boot that sees the account
    /var, /var/log  variable data

    There is no shared /tmp. A session opened through the filesystem
    policy gets a private /tmp that no other session can reach and that
    is removed when the session ends (fs.policy(5)).

    There are no device files. A device is a capability, never a file, so
    creating a file can never create access to hardware.

SEE ALSO
    fs.policy(5), capabilities(7), horus(7), ls(1)
